"""
=========================================================================================
 ML 102 - DRUG ADOPTION CAPSTONE  |  END-TO-END PRODUCTION PIPELINE  (FIXED v4)
=========================================================================================
Objective : Predict which of the 1,502 non-adopted physicians (Test_physicians.csv) will
            adopt drug 'XYZ' in Quarter 11, using 10 quarters of historical transactional
            data (Input_data_file1.csv) and static physician profile data
            (Input_data_file2.csv).

WHAT CHANGED IN v4 (per your latest instructions) - read this first
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. NO SMOTE anywhere. Class imbalance is handled purely via scale_pos_weight (tuned by
   Optuna), which does not fabricate synthetic rows the way SMOTE does.

2. VIF-based feature removal is now RESTRICTED to only physician_gender_* and
   physician_value_tier_* columns. lag1 / lag2 / lag_avg / lag_change columns are NEVER
   removed for having high VIF - they are always kept and handed to the model, exactly
   as you asked ("let model consume them"). Multicollinearity among lag features is not
   a real problem for tree-based models (XGBoost/LightGBM split on one feature at a
   time and are not sensitive to it the way linear/logistic regression is) - the
   VIF check here exists only to clean up genuinely redundant demographic dummies.

3. Feature pruning is now SHAP-driven instead of VIF-driven for everything except
   gender/tier. The pipeline trains an initial ("Stage 1") model on the full feature
   set, computes SHAP importance from BOTH XGBoost and LightGBM, and only drops a
   feature if it is unimportant in BOTH models (below SHAP_IMPORTANCE_MIN_PCT of total
   importance). A "Stage 2" model is then retrained on the reduced, cleaner feature set.

4. physician_id and year_quarter (target_quarter) are NEVER dropped from the dataframe.
   They are excluded only from the FEATURE LIST used to fit the model (which is the
   correct approach - a raw ID or literal quarter label is not something you want a
   tree model splitting on), while remaining present as columns throughout, used for
   grouping, joins, and the time-based train/test split below. Nothing calls
   .drop(columns=[ID_COL]) or .drop(columns=['target_quarter']) anywhere in this script.

5. TIME-BASED SPLIT (replaces the old random 80/20 split):
     - TRAIN : target quarters 3-8   (quarter 1-2 excluded: too little lag history to be
               fully reliable, per your instruction to start at quarter 3)
     - TEST  : target quarters 9-10  (strictly after train, so test performance reflects
               genuine forward-looking / out-of-time generalization)
     - SCORE : quarter 10 used as the most recent lag -> predicts quarter 11 adoption
               for the 1,502 physicians in Test_physicians.csv
   This is a much more honest evaluation than a random split, because it matches how
   the model will actually be used (train on the past, predict a future you haven't
   seen yet).

6. CROSS-VALIDATION METHOD COMPARISON (rolling window vs. StratifiedKFold vs. plain
   KFold): implemented and run explicitly - see compare_cv_strategies(). Rolling-window
   (expanding, time-ordered) CV is used for the real hyperparameter tuning, and here is
   why, made concrete instead of asserted: StratifiedKFold and KFold both shuffle rows
   from quarters 3-8 together at random, so a fold's "validation" data can include
   quarter-3 rows while quarter-8 rows sit in the training fold - i.e. the model gets
   to train on data that is chronologically AFTER some of what it's being validated on.
   That never happens in real deployment (you only ever have the past to predict the
   future), so hyperparameters chosen that way tend to look better in CV than they
   actually perform on true out-of-time data (exactly the quarter 9-10 test set, and
   quarter 11 scoring). Rolling-window CV only ever validates on a quarter that is
   strictly after everything in its training fold, so it is the one CV scheme that is
   methodologically consistent with the outer train(3-8)/test(9-10)/score(11) design.
   The comparison prints actual PR-AUC numbers for all three so this isn't just a
   claim - you can see the gap yourself.

7. THRESHOLD - three things are now printed / produced instead of one silent 0.5 cutoff:
     a. OOF-tuned threshold (F-beta optimized on out-of-fold rolling-CV predictions,
        recent-folds-weighted) - the statistically-grounded choice.
     b. A full threshold-scan table on the OOF data (precision/recall/F1/count at every
        cutoff from 0.05 to 0.95).
     c. A threshold-scan table on the ACTUAL Q11 SCORING POPULATION - since there are no
        true labels for Q11 yet, this table only shows predicted count at each cutoff
        (no precision/recall possible), but it directly answers "what threshold gives me
        250-300 physicians" - you can read it straight off the table.
     d. A "volume-calibrated" threshold is also computed automatically: the exact
        probability cutoff that selects ~275 physicians (midpoint of your 250-300 target)
        from the scoring population, output as its own column alongside the statistically
        tuned prediction, so you have both a principled model-driven answer AND a
        guaranteed-volume business answer in the same file.

8. "Only use a physician's data up to their first adoption quarter" - unchanged from
   v2/v3, verified correct via assert_single_adoption_per_physician(), which hard-fails
   the run if any physician has more than one adoption row in the panel.

DESIGN ASSUMPTIONS (unchanged, still valid):
  A1. A physician is only a valid "non-adopted" training example for target quarter Q if
      brand_prescribed == 0 for every quarter strictly before Q. Once a physician
      adopts, all subsequent quarters are dropped from the panel.
  A2. lag1 = most recent quarter strictly before target quarter; lag2 = second most
      recent. Built via groupby(physician_id).shift() -> the target quarter's own
      values are NEVER used as features -> zero leakage.
  A3. Rows where lag1 is completely unavailable (physician's very first observed
      quarter) are dropped. Rows where only lag2 is unavailable are kept; lag_avg /
      lag_change are left NaN for those rows.
  A4. For scoring Test_physicians.csv (Q11 prediction), lag1 = Quarter 10, lag2 =
      Quarter 9 for every test physician.
  A5. All EDA decisions (missingness threshold, gender mode, VIF) are learned on TRAIN
      ONLY (quarters 3-8) and simply applied to test (9-10) / the Q11 scoring set.

Run order: organized as sequential STEP blocks. Edit FILE PATHS and CONFIG in STEP 0,
run top to bottom.
=========================================================================================
"""

# =========================================================================================
# STEP 0 : IMPORTS & CONFIG
# =========================================================================================
